    [
     1,
     "<div>I would think you could do this via grobs.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nBut I might be wrong and don&#39;t have access to a PC at present to have a play.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nYou might get some hints at <a href=\"https://ikashnitsky.github.io/2017/align-six-maps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://ikashnitsky.github.io/2017/align-six-maps&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792366869826000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1JFkHZBtXoTMrAagjvr2Ut\">https://ikashnitsky.github.io/2017/align-six-maps</a>/ although they are arranging plots on a page rather than on another plot, but I&#39;d think the concept is similar.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhat I&#39;m not sure about is why you would use a bar plot to represent a single data point for each earthquake. Wouldn&#39;t geom_point with colour or size mapped to magnitude be more appropriate? And much simpler to implement.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nRegards,<br>\r\nRon.</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 6 November 2017 06:43:19 GMT-05:00, &quot;白铉&quot; &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">wxx19...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0pt 0pt 0pt 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>在 2017年11月1日星期三 UTC+8上午6:38:12，Hasan Diwan写道：<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I see a possible solution to what I want to do is geom_subplot, but that package seems to be abandoned. What I have is a CSV consisting of latitude, longitude, and magnitudes of earthquakes over the past 2 weeks and I&#39;d like to plot it on a map. What I have so far follows:<div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><span><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\">require(maps)</font></span></p><p><span><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\">require(ggplot2)</font></span></p><p><span><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\">require(ggthemes)</font></span></p><p><span><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\">quake.data &lt;- read.csv(&#39;<a href=\"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/feed/v1.0/summary/all_week.csv\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/feed/v1.0/summary/all_week.csv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792366869826000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0s_Nec80nfx7RwgZv8DaQM\">https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/feed/v1.0/summary/all_week.csv</a>&#39;)</font></span></p><p><span><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\">quake.data &lt;- quake.data[quake.data[,15] == &#39;earthquake&#39;,] # earthquakes only, wtf are ice quakes anyway?</font></span></p><p><span><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\">mapWorld &lt;- borders(&quot;world&quot;, colour=&quot;gray50&quot;, fill=&quot;gray50&quot;) # create a layer of borders</font></span></p><p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n</p><p><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\"><span>ggplot() + mapWorld + geom_point(data = quake.data, aes(x=longitude, y=latitude, colour = mag, size=mag*10)) + ggtitle(&#39;Last week quakes visualised, size of the points is the magnitude&#39;, subtitle=&#39;Source: US Geological Survey&#39;) + theme_minimal()<span>  </span>+ theme(</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">axis.title.x </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">=</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">element_blank</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">(),</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> axis.title.y </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">=</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">element_blank</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">())</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">+</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">scale_colour_gradient2</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">(</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">guide </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">=</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">guide_legend</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">(</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">title </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">=</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\"> </span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">&quot;Magnitude&quot;</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">))</span></font></p><p><font size=\"1\" color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Monaco\">Instead of geom_point, I&#39;d rather use geom_bar to create a bar representing the earthquake&#39;s magnitude. A solution seems to be to use geom_subplot[1], but that package is archived and the package&#39;s most re</span></font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Monaco\" size=\"1\">cent fork doesn&#39;t build on R 3.4.2[2]. Any other vectors perhaps sort this? Many thanks in advance.